                <pb facs="0633" n="539" xml:id="pg539"/>

                <head>Persia:</head>
                <p>[The Department of State had hoped to print in the present volume of <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi> the full record of the case
                    concerning the killing of Vice Consul Robert W. Imbrie at Teheran, Persia, on
                    July 18, 1924. However, when certain documents in the case were, in accordance
                    with established practice, submitted to the Iranian Government with a view to
                    obtaining permission for publication, that Government requested that the
                    documents in question be not published at this time.</p>
                <p>In view of the importance of the Imbrie case from the point of view of
                    international law, the Department considered that it would be undesirable to
                    publish only a part of the record since such publication would detract from a
                    proper understanding of the case. In the circumstances the Department
                    reluctantly reached the conclusion that it would be best to defer publication
                    until such time as the Iranian Government was in a position to give its consent
                    to the publication of the above-mentioned documents, and at that time to publish
                    the entire record.]</p>





